1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
An AI agent can roll back a broken service. The hard question is not whether it can act.
It is who gets to say yes, what is allowed, and whether the result can be proved. This is ArcadeOps.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Inside TrueForge, checkout API is degraded on version forty-two. The mission is narrow: investigate,
verify, and, only if every gate passes, roll back to version forty-one. One write. Nothing broader.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The agent starts with a real MCP inspection: 18.4% errors. TrueForge creates one Verifier assigned
a read-only task. It independently checks the incident and prepares the same rollback: two reads, zero write attempts.

4
00:00:43,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Next, the agent generates a fail-closed Python validator inside a Daytona sandbox. It checks the incident,
service, versions, threshold, and token. Without SANDBOX_VALIDATION_PASS, the workflow stops.

5
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Only then does TrueForge pause the irreversible tool call. The participant explicitly chooses Allow or Deny.
This run persisted one Allow input; its UI activation was delegated, not the decision. No approval is inferred.

6
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:36,000
After approval, exactly one rollback executes. A fresh inspection shows version forty-one healthy at 0.7% errors.
Replay fails, concurrent attempts still permit one write, and out-of-scope actions return Authority Denied.

7
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The Evidence Console turns the persisted session into a Receipt anyone can inspect. Twenty-two checks correlate
the tools, Verifier, Daytona run, approval input, single write, and recovered service. Remove a record and success disappears.

8
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:04,900
TrueForge supplies the harness. ArcadeOps makes authority enforceable.
The agent does the work. The human keeps the decision. The Receipt carries the correlated evidence.
